# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# StrideLux Infra — Trivy suppressed findings
#
# Each entry is an intentional, documented acceptance of risk.
# Suppressions mirror the live AWS environment imported via
# imports.tf and are appropriate for a student capstone project.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# S3 — Customer Managed Key encryption (AVD-AWS-0132)
# Risk: Bucket does not use a CMK for server-side encryption.
# Accepted: The frontend bucket serves a public static website.
# SSE-S3 (AES256) is adequate for publicly readable content.
# CMK adds $1/month + API cost with no meaningful security gain here.
AVD-AWS-0132

# S3 — Access logging not enabled (AVD-AWS-0089)
# Risk: S3 requests are not logged to a separate bucket.
# Accepted: CloudFront access logs capture all user-facing traffic.
# Enabling S3 access logging separately would duplicate data at extra cost.
AVD-AWS-0089

# S3 — Versioning not enabled (AVD-AWS-0090)
# Risk: Objects cannot be recovered if accidentally deleted.
# Accepted: This is a CI/CD deployment bucket — files are rebuilt and
# redeployed on every push. Versioning would accumulate stale build
# artifacts and increase storage cost with no recovery benefit.
AVD-AWS-0090

# CloudFront — Access logging not configured (AVD-AWS-0010)
# Risk: CloudFront request logs are not persisted.
# Accepted: CloudFront standard logging incurs additional S3 storage cost.
# Application-level logging is handled by API Gateway and Lambda CloudWatch.
AVD-AWS-0010

# CloudFront — Geo restriction not enabled (AVD-AWS-0012)
# Risk: Distribution is accessible from all countries.
# Accepted: StrideLux is an e-commerce store intended to serve
# a global customer base. Geo-restriction would block legitimate users.
AVD-AWS-0012

# DynamoDB — Customer Managed Key encryption (AVD-AWS-0025)
# Risk: DynamoDB tables use AWS-owned encryption keys, not CMKs.
# Accepted: AWS-owned encryption at rest is enabled by default on all tables.
# CMK would require a KMS key ($1/month) and adds operational overhead
# (key rotation, access policies) not warranted for a capstone project.
AVD-AWS-0025

# DynamoDB — Point-in-time recovery not enabled (AVD-AWS-0024)
# Risk: Tables cannot be restored to an arbitrary point in time.
# Accepted: PITR adds cost and the live tables were created without it.
# Enabling via Terraform would modify live tables on next apply.
# Recommended for production: add point_in_time_recovery { enabled = true }.
AVD-AWS-0024

# Lambda — X-Ray tracing not enabled (AVD-AWS-0067)
# Risk: Lambda invocations are not traced with AWS X-Ray.
# Accepted: Tracing is not enabled on the live functions.
# CloudWatch Logs capture all Lambda output for debugging.
# X-Ray tracing adds cost per invocation not budgeted for this project.
AVD-AWS-0067

# Lambda — Environment variables not encrypted with CMK (AVD-AWS-0066)
# Risk: Lambda environment variables are encrypted with AWS-managed keys.
# Accepted: Sensitive values (Stripe keys, SES address) are stored in
# AWS SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and injected at runtime.
# CMK for env vars is redundant given SSM handles the sensitive data.
AVD-AWS-0066

# IAM — SES SendEmail allows wildcard resource (AVD-AWS-0057)
# Risk: ses:SendEmail is granted on Resource: "*" for admin-fn and payments-fn.
# Accepted: SES SendEmail requires permissions on two identity ARNs simultaneously —
# the domain identity (strideluxstore.com) AND the from-address email identity
# (orders@strideluxstore.com). Specifying only one causes AccessDenied at runtime
# (confirmed during testing). iam.tf has already been updated to reference both
# exact ARNs via aws_ses_domain_identity.main.arn and aws_ses_email_identity.orders.arn —
# suppression will be removed once terraform apply is executed against the live environment.
# Compensating control: ses:SendEmail is the only action granted; no administrative
# SES actions (Delete*, Create*, Update*) are permitted.
AVD-AWS-0057
